---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Tldr, Eli5, WhyItMatters, HowItWorks, FailureModes, WhatILearned, DeepDive } from '../components/blocks';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Agentic Engineering"
  description="A single prompt gets one shot at an answer. An agent loops — think, act, observe, repeat — until the task is actually done. Watch a real toy agent do exactly that."
>
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/ai/">← AI</a></p>
  <h1>Agentic Engineering</h1>

  <Tldr>
    A plain prompt gets <strong>one shot</strong>: ask, get an answer, done
    — right or wrong. An agent instead runs a <strong>loop</strong>: think
    about what to do next, take an action (usually calling a tool), observe
    what came back, and repeat until the task is actually finished. Most
    real tasks can't be answered in one shot — they need information the
    model doesn't have yet, which means it has to go get it, one step at a
    time, adjusting its plan as it learns more.
  </Tldr>

  <Eli5>
    A single prompt is like asking someone to guess your weight through a
    closed door — one shot, no follow-up, no way to check. An agent is like
    letting them actually open the door, look at the scale, and tell you.
    If the answer depends on more than one thing — say, whoever's
    <em>heaviest</em> out of five people — they need to check each person,
    remember what they've found so far, and keep going until everyone's
    checked. That checking-one-thing-at-a-time-and-remembering loop is the
    entire idea behind an agent. Nothing mystical — just a plan, one real
    action, one real result, repeated until done.
  </Eli5>

  <WhyItMatters>
    <ul>
      <li>Most useful tasks need information gathered <strong>during</strong> the task, not known up front — a single prompt can't do that.</li>
      <li>This is the actual mechanical difference between "an AI that answers questions" and "an AI that gets things done."</li>
      <li>Every real agent framework — regardless of vendor — is built on this same think/act/observe shape underneath.</li>
    </ul>
  </WhyItMatters>

  <HowItWorks>
    <p>Every iteration of the loop has the same three parts:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Thought</strong> — what should happen next, given everything learned so far.</li>
      <li><strong>Action</strong> — a real tool call: a function with real inputs and a real return value, not a guess.</li>
      <li><strong>Observation</strong> — the actual result, which becomes part of what the next Thought reasons about.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The loop ends when the task is done, or when a step budget runs out — see the lab and Failure Modes below for why that budget matters.</p>
  </HowItWorks>

  <section class="lab" aria-labelledby="lab-heading">
    <h2 id="lab-heading" class="eyebrow section-heading">Try it yourself: watch the loop run</h2>
    <p class="lab-note">
      A real toy agent, not a canned animation: it's given five packages
      and a <code>checkWeight(id)</code> tool that returns exactly one
      package's weight per call — it genuinely doesn't know the heaviest
      one until it's checked all five.
    </p>
    <div class="generate-row">
      <button type="button" id="run-btn" class="ex-btn">Run agent →</button>
    </div>
    <div class="trace-log" id="trace-log" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </section>

  <FailureModes>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"More loop iterations always means a better answer."</strong> An agent without a step budget can loop forever on a task that isn't actually solvable the way it's framed — a runaway loop is a real, common failure mode, not a hypothetical one.</li>
      <li><strong>"The agent understands the task."</strong> It's still predicting the next best step from patterns, one step at a time — see How LLMs Predict Text. It can plan badly, misjudge when it's done, or loop on a mistaken assumption with total confidence.</li>
      <li><strong>"Agents replace normal software."</strong> The tools an agent calls are usually just ordinary, deterministic code — the loop is what's new, not the actions inside it.</li>
    </ul>
  </FailureModes>

  <DeepDive>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>This pattern has a name: ReAct</strong> (Reason + Act) —
        interleaving reasoning steps with real tool calls, rather than
        planning everything up front or acting with no reasoning at all.
        It's the shape almost every modern agent framework builds on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Termination conditions matter as much as the loop itself.</strong>
        A real agent needs an explicit way to decide "done": a success
        signal, a maximum step count, or a budget on tool calls or tokens
        — without one, a confused agent just keeps looping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tool schemas are the actual interface.</strong> The model
        doesn't call your code directly — it's given a description of what
        tools exist and what arguments they take (this is exactly what
        the Model Context Protocol standardizes), and it emits a
        structured request that your system executes and feeds back in.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DeepDive>

  <WhatILearned>
    An agent isn't a smarter model — it's the same prediction mechanism
    wrapped in a loop with real tools and a stopping condition. The
    intelligence people notice is mostly the loop doing its job: gather
    information, reassess, and try again, exactly the way a person would.
  </WhatILearned>

  <Sources
    sources={[
      {
        title: 'ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models',
        url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629',
        note: 'The paper that named and formalized the think/act/observe loop this whole page is about.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Model Context Protocol',
        url: 'https://modelcontextprotocol.io',
        note: 'The open standard for how tool schemas actually get described to a model.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Intelligent agent — Wikipedia',
        url: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligent_agent',
        note: 'The broader concept this idea predates LLMs by decades — this is not a new idea in AI, just a newly practical one.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  interface Package { id: string; weightKg: number; }
  interface Step { type: 'thought' | 'action' | 'observation' | 'final'; text: string; }

  const PACKAGES: Package[] = [
    { id: 'A', weightKg: 12 },
    { id: 'B', weightKg: 47 },
    { id: 'C', weightKg: 8 },
    { id: 'D', weightKg: 33 },
    { id: 'E', weightKg: 41 },
  ];

  // The "tool": returns exactly one package's weight per call, same as a
  // real API the agent can't see all the results of at once.
  function checkWeight(id: string): number | null {
    return PACKAGES.find((p) => p.id === id)?.weightKg ?? null;
  }

  function* runAgent(): Generator<Step> {
    yield { type: 'thought', text: `Task: find the heaviest of ${PACKAGES.length} packages. I can only check one at a time.` };
    let best: { id: string; weightKg: number } | null = null;
    for (const pkg of PACKAGES) {
      yield { type: 'action', text: `checkWeight("${pkg.id}")` };
      const weight = checkWeight(pkg.id)!;
      yield { type: 'observation', text: `${weight} kg` };
      if (!best || weight > best.weightKg) {
        best = { id: pkg.id, weightKg: weight };
        yield { type: 'thought', text: `New heaviest so far: ${pkg.id} at ${weight} kg.` };
      } else {
        yield { type: 'thought', text: `${pkg.id} (${weight} kg) is lighter than current best (${best.id}, ${best.weightKg} kg). Continuing.` };
      }
    }
    yield { type: 'final', text: `Checked all ${PACKAGES.length} packages. Heaviest: ${best!.id} at ${best!.weightKg} kg.` };
  }

  const SCRAMBLE_GLYPHS = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789#$%&*+=<>/\\';
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
  const log = document.getElementById('trace-log')!;
  const runBtn = document.getElementById('run-btn') as HTMLButtonElement;
  let runTimer: number | undefined;

  const LABELS: Record<Step['type'], string> = {
    thought: 'THOUGHT', action: 'ACTION', observation: 'OBSERVATION', final: 'FINAL',
  };

  function scrambleInto(el: HTMLElement, finalText: string, frames = 6, frameMs = 30, onDone?: () => void) {
    if (prefersReducedMotion) {
      el.textContent = finalText;
      onDone?.();
      return;
    }
    let frame = 0;
    const timer = window.setInterval(() => {
      frame++;
      if (frame > frames) {
        clearInterval(timer);
        el.textContent = finalText;
        onDone?.();
        return;
      }
      el.textContent = Array.from({ length: finalText.length }, () => SCRAMBLE_GLYPHS[Math.floor(Math.random() * SCRAMBLE_GLYPHS.length)]).join('');
    }, frameMs);
  }

  function run() {
    if (runTimer) { clearTimeout(runTimer); runTimer = undefined; }
    log.innerHTML = '';
    runBtn.disabled = true;
    const steps = runAgent();

    const next = () => {
      const { value: step, done } = steps.next();
      if (done) { runBtn.disabled = false; return; }
      const row = document.createElement('div');
      row.className = `trace-row trace-${step.type}`;
      const label = document.createElement('span');
      label.className = 'trace-label';
      label.textContent = LABELS[step.type];
      const text = document.createElement('span');
      text.className = 'trace-text';
      row.append(label, text);
      log.append(row);
      log.scrollTop = log.scrollHeight;

      if (step.type === 'observation') {
        scrambleInto(text, step.text, 6, 30, () => { runTimer = window.setTimeout(next, 220); });
      } else {
        text.textContent = step.text;
        runTimer = window.setTimeout(next, step.type === 'final' ? 0 : 350);
      }
    };
    next();
  }

  runBtn.addEventListener('click', run);
</script>

<style>
  .lab { margin: var(--sp-6) 0; padding: var(--sp-4); border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--bg-1); }
  .section-heading { margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  .lab-note { color: var(--text-2); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); }
  .generate-row { margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); }
  .ex-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); color: var(--text-1); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .ex-btn:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--text-0); }
  .ex-btn:disabled { opacity: 0.5; cursor: default; }

  .trace-log {
    min-height: 8rem; max-height: 22rem; overflow-y: auto; padding: var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright);
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); display: grid; gap: var(--sp-2);
  }
  .trace-row { display: flex; gap: var(--sp-3); }
  .trace-label { flex-shrink: 0; width: 6.5rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; font-weight: 700; }
  .trace-thought .trace-label { color: var(--text-2); }
  .trace-thought .trace-text { color: var(--text-1); }
  .trace-action .trace-label { color: var(--accent); }
  .trace-action .trace-text { color: var(--text-0); }
  .trace-observation .trace-label { color: var(--accent); }
  .trace-observation .trace-text { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; text-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--accent), 0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 60%, transparent); }
  .trace-final .trace-label { color: var(--status-working); }
  .trace-final .trace-text { color: var(--status-working); font-weight: 700; }
</style>
